        "original_id": "f8be15a9-6f04-57e9-afcb-d8398eec2eb7",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Herbie Flowers, Paul McCartney, Elton John, David Bowie, Lou Reed, Melanie, Roy Harper, David Essex, Allan Clarke, Al Kooper, Bryan Ferry, Harry Nilsson, Cat Stevens, Serge Gainsbourg, George Harrison, Ringo Starr\n- Groups: Blue Mink, T. Rex, Sky\n- Albums: Tumbleweed Connection, Madman Across the Water, Space Oddity, Diamond Dogs, Transformer, Candles in the Rain, Bullinamingvase, Rock On, New York City (You're a Woman), The Bride Stripped Bare, Nilsson Schmilsson, Son of Dracula, New Masters, Foreigner, Histoire de Melody Nelson, Give My Regards to Broad Street, Somewhere in England, Gone Troppo, Brainwashed, Stop and Smell the Roses\n- Songs: Walk on the Wild Side\n- Roles: Session bass players\n- Films: Give My Regards to Broad Street\n\nFacts:\n- Herbie Flowers was a member of Blue Mink, T. Rex, and Sky.\n- Herbie Flowers became one of Britain's best-known session bass players.\n- Herbie Flowers contributed bass to recordings by Elton John, including the albums \"Tumbleweed Connection\" and \"Madman Across the Water.\"\n- Herbie Flowers contributed bass to recordings by David Bowie, including the albums \"Space Oddity\" and \"Diamond Dogs.\"\n- Herbie Flowers contributed bass to Lou Reed's album \"Transformer,\" including the prominent bass line on the song \"Walk on the Wild Side.\"\n- Herbie Flowers contributed bass to recordings by Melanie, Roy Harper, David Essex, Allan Clarke, Al Kooper, Bryan Ferry, Harry Nilsson, Cat Stevens, and Serge Gainsbourg.\n- Herbie Flowers contributed bass to Paul McCartney's album \"Give My Regards to Broad Street.\"\n- Paul McCartney's \"Give My Regards to Broad Street\" was his fifth studio album.\n- \"Give My Regards to Broad Street\" was also the soundtrack album to Paul McCartney's 1984 film of the same name.\n- Herbie Flowers also contributed bass to recordings by George Harrison, including \"Somewhere in England,\" \"Gone Troppo,\" and \"Brainwashed,\" and by Ringo Starr on \"Stop and Smell the Roses.\"",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
